1. What is port?

Think of an IP address as a building's address, and a port as a specific room inside that building.

IP Address identifies the computer.
Port Number identifies the application or service running on that computer.

Without ports, the operating system would not know which application should receive the incoming data.

2. Real-Life Example

Suppose a company has this address:

ABC Company
Mumbai

Inside the company there are different departments:

HR
Accounts
IT
Sales

If someone visits the company, they must know which department they want.

Similarly:

IP Address → Computer
Port → Application/Service

3. Example

Your Linux server has the IP:

192.168.1.100

Running services:

Service	Port
SSH	22
HTTP	80
HTTPS	443
MySQL	3306

When you connect to:

192.168.1.100:22

the operating system sends your request to the SSH service.

If you connect to:

192.168.1.100:80

the request goes to the web server.

4. Port Number Range

Ports range from:

0 – 65535

These are divided into three categories.

a) Well-Known Ports (0–1023)

Reserved for common services.

| Port | Service |
| ---- | ------- |
| 22   | SSH     |
| 53   | DNS     |
| 80   | HTTP    |
| 443  | HTTPS   |

b) Registered Ports (1024–49151)

Used by applications.

| Port | Application |
| ---- | ----------- |
| 3306 | MySQL       |
| 5432 | PostgreSQL  |
| 6379 | Redis       |
| 8080 | Tomcat      |

c) Dynamic / Ephemeral Ports (49152–65535)

Temporary ports used by clients.

Example:

Your browser opens a temporary port to connect to Google.

Once communication ends,

the port is closed automatically.

==> Linux Commands

Check Listening Ports
ss -tuln

Meaning
-t  TCP
-u  UDP
-l  Listening
-n  Show numbers (don't resolve names)

Output:

LISTEN

0.0.0.0:22

0.0.0.0:80

0.0.0.0:443

Meaning SSH, HTTP, HTTPS are running.

==> Older Command
netstat -tuln

Same purpose.

Nowadays,

ss is preferred because it is faster.

==> Check Which Process is Using a Port
sudo lsof -i :22

Output

sshd

Meaning

SSH service is using Port 22.

==> Check Open Port on Another Server
nc -zv 192.168.1.100 22

Output

Connection successful

Meaning

Port 22 is open.

==> Scan Open Ports
nmap 192.168.1.100

Output

22 open
80 open
443 open

Very useful during troubleshooting.

Note:-

You deploy a web application on an EC2 instance.

Users report that the website is not opening.

You troubleshoot in this order:

step 1: Check if the web server is running:

sudo systemctl status nginx

step 2: Check if Nginx is listening on port 80 or 443:

ss -tuln

step 3: Verify the firewall allows the required ports.

sudo ufw status

output:
Status: active

22/tcp    ALLOW
80/tcp    ALLOW
443/tcp   ALLOW

sometimes If you only see:

22/tcp    ALLOW

then Port 80 is blocked, so users cannot open the website.
a) ubuntu:
To allow Port 80
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp

For HTTPS:

sudo ufw allow 443/tcp

then reload the firewall
sudo ufw reload

b) On RHEL/CentOS (firewalld)

Check firewall rules:

sudo firewall-cmd --list-all

If Port 80 is missing, add it:

sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

step 4: Check the AWS Security Group

This step is only for AWS.

A Security Group is like a firewall, but it works at the AWS cloud level.

Even if your Linux firewall allows Port 80, users still cannot access the website if the AWS Security Group blocks it.

Example

Suppose your EC2 instance has:

Nginx running ✅
Port 80 listening ✅
Linux firewall allows Port 80 ✅

But your website still doesn't open.

You check the AWS Security Group and find:

Inbound Rules

SSH (22) ✔
HTTP (80) ❌ Not Allowed
HTTPS (443) ❌ Not Allowed

Since HTTP isn't allowed, AWS blocks the request before it even reaches your server.

How to Fix It
Open the AWS Console.
Go to EC2.
Select your EC2 Instance.
Click the attached Security Group.
Go to Inbound Rules.
Add a new rule:
Type	Port	Source
HTTP	80	0.0.0.0/0

For HTTPS:

Type	Port	Source
HTTPS	443	0.0.0.0/0

Save the rules.

Now users can access your website.
step 5: Test locally:

curl http://localhost

step 6: Test remotely:

curl http://<server-ip>
